Steps    | Final score  | Clean-square credit
------------------------------------------------
2        | 9            | 2
5        | 17           | 8
10       | 12           | 18
20       | 2            | 38
50       | -28          | 98
100      | -78          | 198

The score falls by 1 for every step after the world is clean:
the reflex agent oscillates A -&gt; B -&gt; A -&gt; ... for ever.

================================================================================================
&gt;&gt;&gt; RUN 4 : control - can a different rule table fix it without memory?
            rules: Dirty -&gt; Suck, Clean -&gt; NoOp;  world: only B dirty

Initial state : A=Clean  B=Dirty  agent@A
Performance   : score = 0

Step  | Percept          | Action | Resulting world state     | Reward  | Score  | CleanCredit
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1     | (A, Clean)       | NoOp   | A=Clean  B=Dirty  agent@A |      +0 |     +0 | 1
2     | (A, Clean)       | NoOp   | A=Clean  B=Dirty  agent@A |      +0 |     +0 | 2
3     | (A, Clean)       | NoOp   | A=Clean  B=Dirty  agent@A |      +0 |     +0 | 3
4     | (A, Clean)       | NoOp   | A=Clean  B=Dirty  agent@A |      +0 |     +0 | 4
5     | (A, Clean)       | NoOp   | A=Clean  B=Dirty  agent@A |      +0 |     +0 | 5
6     | (A, Clean)       | NoOp   | A=Clean  B=Dirty  agent@A |      +0 |     +0 | 6
7     | (A, Clean)       | NoOp   | A=Clean  B=Dirty  agent@A |      +0 |     +0 | 7
8     | (A, Clean)       | NoOp   | A=Clean  B=Dirty  agent@A |      +0 |     +0 | 8
9     | (A, Clean)       | NoOp   | A=Clean  B=Dirty  agent@A |      +0 |     +0 | 9
10    | (A, Clean)       | NoOp   | A=Clean  B=Dirty  agent@A |      +0 |     +0 | 10

Final state   : A=Clean  B=Dirty  agent@A
Performance   : score = 0   (clean-square credit = 10 over 10 steps)

It stops paying the movement cost, but it never reaches B either:
B stays dirty for ever.  The percept (A, Clean) has to be mapped to
exactly one action, and the same percept occurs both when the world
is finished and when B is still dirty.  No memoryless rule table can
be right in both cases - the limitation is the architecture, not the
choice of rules.</pre><br style='page-break-before:always'><h2>Observations</h2><h3>1. Why the agent keeps moving after both squares are clean</h3><p>The agent has exactly one input, the percept of the square it stands on, and that percept does not mention the other square. At step 5 of RUN 1 the agent perceives <span class=m>(A, Clean)</span>; it perceived exactly the same thing at step 2, when B was still dirty. Nothing in the agent distinguishes the two situations, so it must take the same action in both. Rule R2 says <i>Right</i>, and it says <i>Right</i> for ever: the agent walks A &rarr; B &rarr; A &rarr; B, paying &minus;1 a step for work it has already finished.</p><p>RUN 4 is the control that shows this is not a bad choice of rules. Replacing R2 and R3 with &quot;if Clean then NoOp&quot; does stop the oscillation &mdash; and in a world where only B is dirty the agent then sits on the clean square A for all ten steps, scores 0 and leaves B dirty for ever. One memoryless table wastes energy; the other never finishes the job. Since both worlds present the identical percept <span class=m>(A, Clean)</span>, no memoryless table can be correct in both. The limitation is architectural.</p><h3>2. How the performance score changes with the number of steps</h3><p>RUN 3 measures this directly. Starting from both squares dirty, the agent needs three steps to clean the world (Suck, Right, Suck) and reaches its maximum score of +19 at step 3. Every step after that is a movement, so for a horizon of <i>n</i> &ge; 3 steps the final score is exactly</p><p class=eq>score(n) = 19 &minus; (n &minus; 3)</p><p>which is +12 at n = 10, +2 at n = 20, &minus;28 at n = 50 and &minus;78 at n = 100. The score crosses zero at n = 22 and then falls without bound: the agent's lifetime performance is a strictly decreasing function of how long it is allowed to live, because it has no way to recognise that it is done. Under the PEAS-style measure the picture inverts &mdash; <span class=m>CleanCredit</span> grows by +2 per step once the world is clean, so the same behaviour looks perfect. Which agent is &quot;good&quot; is decided by the performance measure, not by the agent; this is the point of insisting that rationality is always relative to a stated measure.</p><h3>3. What this says about the need for internal state</h3><p>The agent's trouble is not that its rules are wrong but that its percept is smaller than the state it needs to act on. The world has four states (A/B &times; the two dirt bits); the agent can distinguish only two percepts per location. Two bits of memory &mdash; a remembered status for A and for B &mdash; close that gap exactly, and Exercise 1 measures what they are worth: +19 against +2 over twenty steps, with the gap widening by one point per additional step for ever. That is the step from the reflex agent to the model-based reflex agent, and it is the smallest amount of state that buys anything at all.</p><br style='page-break-before:always'><h2>Exercise 1 &mdash; Model-based variant, compared over 20 steps</h2><p><b>Task.</b> Add a model-based variant that remembers the status of both squares and executes a no-op once everything is known to be clean; compare scores over 20 steps.</p><p><b>Approach.</b> The agent becomes an object rather than a pure function, because it now carries state between calls. Its model is a dictionary <span class=m>{A: Unknown, B: Unknown}</span>, filled in only from percepts the agent has actually received &mdash; it never reads the environment. When the agent sucks it records the square as Clean, since it knows the effect of its own action; this is the &quot;model of how the world evolves&quot; half of the model-based architecture. The rules then fire on the model rather than on the bare percept, which is what makes the NoOp decision possible.</p><h3>Program &mdash; exp1_ex1_model_based.py</h3><pre class=code style='font-size:7.5pt'>&quot;&quot;&quot;
Experiment 1 - Exercise 1
Model-based reflex agent : remembers the status of both squares and executes a
no-op once everything is known to be clean.  Scores are compared over 20 steps.

Agentic AI (IA462), SVNIT Surat.  Standard library only; no agent framework.
&quot;&quot;&quot;

from exp1_reflex_agent import (
    VacuumEnvironment, simple_reflex_agent,
    CLEAN, DIRTY, SUCK, LEFT, RIGHT, NOOP,
)


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# The model-based agent.  It is NOT a pure function: it carries a model of the
# world between calls.  The model is built only from percepts it has actually
# received - it never reads the environment&#x27;s private state.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
class ModelBasedReflexAgent:
    &quot;&quot;&quot;Reflex rules + internal state (a belief about both squares).&quot;&quot;&quot;

    def __init__(self):
        # &quot;Unknown&quot; until the agent has visited the square itself.
        self.model = {&quot;A&quot;: &quot;Unknown&quot;, &quot;B&quot;: &quot;Unknown&quot;}

    def __call__(self, percept):
        location, status = percept

        # ---- update internal state from the percept --------------------- #
        self.model[location] = status

        # ---- condition-action rules over the MODEL, not just the percept - #
        if status == DIRTY:
            self.model[location] = CLEAN     # sucking will clean it
            return SUCK
        if all(v == CLEAN for v in self.model.values()):
            return NOOP                      # nothing left to do: stop moving
        return RIGHT if location == &quot;A&quot; else LEFT

    def dump(self):
        return &quot;{A: %s, B: %s}&quot; % (self.model[&quot;A&quot;], self.model[&quot;B&quot;])


def run_agent(env, agent, steps, label, show_model=False):
    print(&quot;%s&quot; % label)
    header = &quot;%-5s | %-16s | %-6s | %-25s | %-6s | %s&quot; % (
        &quot;Step&quot;, &quot;Percept&quot;, &quot;Action&quot;, &quot;Resulting world state&quot;, &quot;Score&quot;,
        &quot;Agent&#x27;s internal model&quot; if show_model else &quot;CleanCredit&quot;)
    print(header)
    print(&quot;-&quot; * len(header))
    for t in range(1, steps + 1):
        percept = env.percept()
        action = agent(percept)
        env.execute(action)
        env.tick_clean_credit()
        tail = agent.dump() if show_model else str(env.clean_credit)
        print(&quot;%-5d | %-16s | %-6s | %-25s | %+6d | %s&quot; % (
            t, &quot;(%s, %s)&quot; % percept, action, env.world_state(),
            env.score, tail))
    print(&quot;Final : %s   score = %+d   clean-square credit = %d&quot;
          % (env.world_state(), env.score, env.clean_credit))
    print()
    return env.score, env.clean_credit


if __name__ == &quot;__main__&quot;:
    STEPS = 20
    START = {&quot;A&quot;: DIRTY, &quot;B&quot;: DIRTY}

    print(&quot;=&quot; * 100)
    print(&quot;EXERCISE 1 - SIMPLE REFLEX  vs  MODEL-BASED REFLEX  (20 steps)&quot;)
    print(&quot;=&quot; * 100)
    print()

    env_a = VacuumEnvironment(status=START, location=&quot;A&quot;)
    s_reflex, c_reflex = run_agent(
        env_a, simple_reflex_agent, STEPS,
        &quot;&gt;&gt;&gt; A. Simple reflex agent (no internal state)&quot;)

    env_b = VacuumEnvironment(status=START, location=&quot;A&quot;)
    s_model, c_model = run_agent(
        env_b, ModelBasedReflexAgent(), STEPS,
        &quot;&gt;&gt;&gt; B. Model-based reflex agent (remembers both squares)&quot;,
        show_model=True)

    print(&quot;=&quot; * 100)
    print(&quot;COMPARISON OVER %d STEPS&quot; % STEPS)
    print(&quot;=&quot; * 100)
    print(&quot;%-32s | %-12s | %s&quot; % (&quot;Agent&quot;, &quot;Task score&quot;, &quot;Clean-square credit&quot;))
    print(&quot;-&quot; * 74)
    print(&quot;%-32s | %-12d | %d&quot; % (&quot;Simple reflex&quot;, s_reflex, c_reflex))
    print(&quot;%-32s | %-12d | %d&quot; % (&quot;Model-based reflex&quot;, s_model, c_model))
    print(&quot;-&quot; * 74)
    print(&quot;%-32s | %-12d | %d&quot; % (&quot;Difference (model - reflex)&quot;,
                                  s_model - s_reflex, c_model - c_reflex))
    print()
    print(&quot;Both agents clean the world in the same first three steps.  After&quot;)
    print(&quot;that the simple reflex agent pays -1 per step for ever, while the&quot;)
    print(&quot;model-based agent recognises that its model shows both squares&quot;)
    print(&quot;Clean and issues NoOp at zero cost.&quot;)</pre><br style='page-break-before:always'><h3>Output</h3><pre class=out style='font-size:6.5pt'>====================================================================================================
