Lazy (never moves)             | 37.85            | 30.00

========================================================================================================
SENSITIVITY TO p  (mean task score / mean clean-square credit, 200 runs of 30 steps)
========================================================================================================
p      | Simple reflex (no state) | Model-based + NoOp  | Model-based + decay(3) | Lazy (never moves) 
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
0.00   |   -8.00 / 58.00     |   19.00 / 58.00     |   12.00 / 58.00     |   10.00 / 30.00    
0.02   |    3.44 / 57.41     |   23.20 / 50.74     |   21.68 / 56.67     |   14.85 / 30.00    
0.05   |   21.20 / 56.42     |   32.15 / 44.09     |   36.39 / 54.48     |   23.60 / 30.00    
0.10   |   47.16 / 54.53     |   45.65 / 38.38     |   58.03 / 51.66     |   37.85 / 30.00    
0.20   |   97.22 / 50.20     |   75.35 / 33.99     |   98.12 / 46.19     |   68.45 / 30.00    
0.40   |  163.54 / 41.22     |  133.70 / 31.50     |  155.67 / 38.03     |  128.30 / 30.00    

Best agent by task score, and by clean-square credit, at each p:
p      | best task score            | best clean-square credit
----------------------------------------------------------------------
0.00   | Model-based + NoOp (19.0)  | Simple reflex (no state) (58.0)
0.02   | Model-based + NoOp (23.2)  | Simple reflex (no state) (57.4)
0.05   | Model-based + decay(3) (36.4) | Simple reflex (no state) (56.4)
0.10   | Model-based + decay(3) (58.0) | Simple reflex (no state) (54.5)
0.20   | Model-based + decay(3) (98.1) | Simple reflex (no state) (50.2)
0.40   | Simple reflex (no state) (163.5) | Simple reflex (no state) (41.2)

Reading the table: at p = 0 the memoryless agent is dominated - it
pays -1 for ever.  As p grows, patrolling starts to pay for itself,
because a square the agent never revisits cannot be cleaned.  The
agent that only *believes* the world is clean is the one that fails:
its model is correct when written and wrong one step later.  Note
also that the do-nothing agent beats the reflex agent at small p:
the reflex agent is not rational there either, because it pays to
move without weighing that cost against the chance of finding dirt.</pre><h3>Discussion &mdash; is the reflex agent rational now?</h3><p>A rational agent is one that, for each percept sequence, selects the action expected to maximise the performance measure, given the evidence of its percepts and whatever knowledge it has built in. Rationality is therefore relative to four things &mdash; the measure, the environment, the actuators and the sensors &mdash; and three of those have just changed. The honest answer the numbers support is: <b>the reflex agent is no longer dominated, but it is still not rational.</b></p><p><b>What improved.</b> In the static world, movement after cleaning was pure waste. In the stochastic world the agent's sensors are local, so moving is the only way to <i>find out</i> whether the other square has become dirty: patrolling is now sensing as well as travelling, and it pays for itself. Over 200 runs of 30 steps the reflex agent goes from being 17 points behind the model-based NoOp agent at p = 0 to ahead of it by p = 0.10 (47.16 against 45.65), and far ahead at p = 0.40 (163.54 against 133.70). On the PEAS measure, cleanliness of the world per time step, the reflex agent is the best of the four at every non-zero p.</p><p><b>What did not improve.</b> The reflex agent still never weighs the &minus;1 cost of a move against the probability of finding dirt at the other end. At small p that is a real error, and the lazy baseline exposes it: at p = 0.02 doing nothing scores 14.85 while patrolling scores 3.44. An agent that walks back and forth to check a square that is dirty two per cent of the time is not behaving rationally, it is merely behaving. A rational policy here would depend on p and on the ratio of the suck reward to the movement cost, and would need to be derived, not guessed &mdash; which is the decision-theoretic agent, not the reflex agent.</p><p><b>The most interesting casualty is memory.</b> The model-based agent that won Exercise 1 is the worst performer on cleanliness at every non-zero p (38.38 at p = 0.10 against the reflex agent's 54.53). Its model is not wrong when it is written; it goes stale, and the agent keeps acting on it, sitting still and issuing NoOp while both squares quietly turn dirty behind its back. Adding a three-step decay to the same belief fixes it and produces the best agent in the table over the whole middle range of p (58.03 at p = 0.10). The lesson is that internal state is only an asset if the agent also knows how fast it rots &mdash; in a dynamic environment a model needs a refresh policy, and that is precisely the sensing/updating machinery the later experiments build.</p><br style='page-break-before:always'><h2>Conclusion</h2><p>A simple reflex agent for the vacuum-cleaner world was implemented from first principles: an environment owning the true state and exposing only <span class=m>percept()</span> and <span class=m>execute(action)</span>, an agent that is a pure function from percept to action, and a perception&ndash;action loop connecting the two. The agent cleans both squares in three steps and reaches its peak score of +19, after which it oscillates for ever and loses one point per step, ending at +12 after 10 steps and &minus;78 after 100. A control run confirmed that no memoryless rule table avoids this: the alternative rules stop the oscillation only by never reaching the other square.</p><p>Three exercises quantified what the missing state is worth. Two bits of memory and a NoOp rule turn +2 into +19 over twenty steps (Exercise 1). In a 1 &times; N corridor started from the middle, a single heading bit is the difference between finishing the job and leaving squares permanently dirty (Exercise 2). When dirt returns with probability p, patrolling becomes sensing and the memoryless agent stops being dominated, while the agent with an un-refreshed model becomes the worst of the four; a belief with a decay rule is best (Exercise 3).</p><p>The general point carried into the later experiments is that agent architecture, performance measure and environment cannot be judged separately. The same agent is excellent under one measure and terrible under another, and the same memory is an asset in a static world and a liability in a dynamic one.</p><h2>Execution environment and declaration</h2><table class=g><tr><th style='width:22%'>Item</th><th style='width:78%'>Detail</th></tr><tr><td>Execution</td><td>Python 3.12.1 on Windows (AMD64), executed 13 August 2026, 00:25</td></tr><tr><td>Files</td><td>exp1_reflex_agent.py, exp1_ex1_model_based.py, exp1_ex2_corridor.py, exp1_ex3_stochastic_dirt.py, make_report.py</td></tr><tr><td>Libraries used</td><td>Python standard library only (<span class=m>random</span>, <span class=m>subprocess</span>, <span class=m>html</span>, <span class=m>datetime</span>). No agent framework or orchestration library was used, in accordance with clause 3.1 of the Rules and Regulations.</td></tr><tr><td>Reproducibility</td><td>All random worlds are seeded (corridor seed 7; stochastic runs seeds 1000&ndash;1199), so every figure in this record reproduces exactly. Removing the seed varies the dirt layout but not the conclusions.</td></tr><tr><td>Sources consulted</td><td>S. Russell and P. Norvig, <i>Artificial Intelligence: A Modern Approach</i>, chapter 2 (agents, PEAS, rationality, the vacuum world); course manual for Experiment 1.</td></tr></table><p class=note>All output reproduced above was produced by executing the programs listed in this record on the machine and at the time stated in the table.</p></div></body>
